data.content:
Here's the full migration. Your code has three patterns that break on .NET 8:

| Pattern Found | Problem on .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown — cleanup code silently skipped |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking calls can wake up
                ProcessItem(item);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — roll back partial work, then exit
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)); // replaces Sleep + Abort
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

1. **`volatile bool _running` → `CancellationToken`** — The token is thread-safe by design and can also unblock waiting calls.

2. **`Thread.Abort()` → `_cts.Cancel()`** — Cooperative: the loop checks `token.IsCancellationRequested` each iteration.

3. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`** — The `when` guard ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not someone else's.

4. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` → removed** — Not needed; after catching `OperationCanceledException`, execution continues normally (into `finally`).

5. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Join(2s)`** — Actually waits for the thread to finish rather than hoping it stopped.

## Critical Pitfall: `GetNextItem` Must Accept the Token

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue or I/O), cancellation **won't take effect** until that call returns. You must thread the token through:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework) — blocks indefinitely, relies on Abort to interrupt
var item = queue.Dequeue(); // or socket.Receive(), etc.

// After (.NET 8) — wakes up on cancellation
var item = queue.Take(token);              // BlockingCollection<T>
var item = await channel.ReadAsync(token); // System.Threading.Channels
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout);         // replaces Thread.Sleep
```

## Other Pitfalls to Watch For

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Use `IDisposable` as shown. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Without the `when` guard, you swallow unrelated cancellations. |
| **Large work items between checks** | Cancellation only fires at checkpoints. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running. |
| **Forgetting `finally` still runs** | Unlike `ThreadAbortException` (which auto-rethrows after catch), `OperationCanceledException` stays caught — your `finally` runs cleanly without `ResetAbort`. |
